{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_98","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git show 9f9eeef:docs/02-product-spec.md | nl -ba | sed -n '189,215p;257,281p' && git show 9f9eeef:docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md | nl -ba | sed -n '105,148p;169,205p;338,348p'\"","aggregated_output":"   189\t### 3.6 Type matrix\n   190\t\n   191\t| | Line | Area | Bar | Donut | Scatter |\n   192\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   193\t| Picking by pointer | Nearest x | Nearest x | The band under the pointer | The slice under the pointer | Nearest point within 24 px; ties go to the later row |\n   194\t| Tooltip content | Every visible series at that x | Same, plus the stack total | Every series in that category | Label, value, percentage | The point's series, x and y; coincident points listed, up to eight |\n   195\t| Keyboard order | By x, then series | By x, then series | By category, then series | By slice order | By x, then y |\n   196\t| Zoom | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | No | Yes, both axes later; x only in 1.0 |\n   197\t| Simplified drawing when dense | F5 | F5 on cumulative sums | No; more bars than pixels raises a hint | No | No in 1.0; above 50,000 points a hint says drawing may be slow |\n   198\t| Limit that raises a diagnostic | Eight series | Eight series | Eight series | Eight slices | Eight series |\n   199\t| Reference lines and bands | Yes; included in the domain; listed in the summary and table caption | Yes | Yes | No | Yes |\n   200\t\n   201\t### 3.7 Compact variant\n   202\t\n   203\t`compact: true` on line, area and bar draws the marks and nothing else: no axes, gridlines, legend, toolbar, table control, zoom or tooltip. It is for a small tile where the page already shows the number and the label.\n   204\t\n   205\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by |\n   206\t|---|---|---|\n   207\t| C1 | A compact chart is a static image with a full text alternative: `role=\"img\"`, an accessible name (as in A1, including AX140 when none is given) and the generated summary as its description (series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, omissions). It has no tab stop and no pointer-only behavior, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. | Accessibility-tree snapshot; a page of 40 has zero added tab stops |\n   208\t| C2 | The fidelity rules still hold: zero baseline for bar and area (F1), gaps (F2), diagnostics (F3), simplified drawing (F5), sorting (F11), no motion (I5), scheme from the page (L4). | The same fixtures as the full charts |\n   209\t| C3 | It fills its container at any size from 24×16 CSS px up; with no definite height it is 32 px tall. | Size tests |\n   210\t| C4 | With more than one series, lines differ by dash as well as color, since there is no legend; AX131 suggests one series per compact chart. | Two-series fixture |\n   211\t| C5 | When values were left out (F12), the description says so and a small marker with a text equivalent appears in the corner. | `dirty` fixture in compact mode |\n   212\t\n   213\tA compact chart does not give access to every value; the summary is its alternative. An author who needs every value reachable uses the full chart.\n   214\t\n   215\t### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\n   257\t## 6. Decisions and the findings behind them\n   258\t\n   259\t\"Status\" records what the reviews changed.\n   260\t\n   261\t| Decision | What decided it | Status after review |\n   262\t|---|---|---|\n   263\t| Diagnostics are a first-class feature with codes | R5: none of the ten named the field, the row or the problem. | Kept. Severity and delivery rewritten (3.5): readers are told too, and production builds are not silent. |\n   264\t| Calendar dates, instants and wall-clock times are different types | R3: given instants, six libraries show a daily value on a different day in some zones, which is correct for instants and wrong for dates; given date strings, most of them get it right. The gap is that nothing says which one the author meant. | Kept, sharpened. The test now separates the types, and a hint fires when instants look like dates. Default zone for instants: the viewer's, with the zone always visible. |\n   265\t| Accessibility is in the core, on by default | R11–R13. | Kept. The table is built on demand, not eagerly. |\n   266\t| The normalized data, not the drawing, is the source for values, table, keyboard and summary | Reviews: a simplified drawing cannot return the values it dropped. | New. |\n   267\t| No animation on first render | Reading starts at first paint, and with 24 charts loading at once motion competes with it. R14: four of seven animating libraries ignore reduced motion. | Kept, with the rationale restated. Update transitions stay capped at 200 ms. |\n   268\t| Label fitting is a tested invariant with a visible fallback | R2. | Changed. The absolute \"everything always fits\" was infeasible in a 240 px tile; it is now \"nothing overlaps, and nothing disappears without the reader being told\". Rotation comes before truncation. |\n   269\t| One logical table, three input forms | D2. | Changed from \"one data shape\": typed columns and wide rows are first-class. |\n   270\t| Three methods with a commit contract | D3. | Changed from \"one update method\": `append` and `zoom` were always there, and their ordering is now specified. |\n   271\t| Canvas marks with DOM text | D9: a canvas library is the speed reference. D10: one DOM node per point is slow. | **Reopened.** Phase 1 does not show that SVG is slow, only that one node per point is; a single SVG path per series was fast. M1 builds both mark renderers on the same scene and measures them (section 8). Canvas is the working assumption. |\n   272\t| Simplified drawing that preserves extremes, on by default | D8. | Kept for lines and areas, with the guarantee restated per pixel column and per run of valid samples. \"Every spike survives\" was wrong as written. |\n   273\t| Palette held to measurable thresholds | R7. | Kept as a screen, marked provisional. Marks carry a non-color cue in every mode, which the first draft did not require. |\n   274\t| Colors and fonts from CSS | R10. | Kept. Repaint triggers specified; `palette` option replaced by `colors` keyed by series name. |\n   275\t| Numeric strings | R5. | Changed: a whole column of plain decimal strings is parsed with a hint; mixed columns are not. |\n   276\t| Chart types | Judgment, then the owner's decision. | All five in 1.0, plus the compact variant (3.7). |\n   277\t| MIT, including zoom and accessibility | D7. | Kept. |\n   278\t| React adapter in 1.0 | Recharts leads npm installs among chart-level libraries. | Built early to test the core API, reviewed twice, then removed from 1.0 by the owner on 2026-11-18 to keep the first release plain TypeScript. Its last state is at commit dac5664 (`packages/react`), the starting point when adapters for React, Vue and Svelte are added. |\n   279\t| Budgets apply to defaults | D8. | Kept. The ratio to uPlot is no longer a gate. |\n   280\t| Zero runtime dependencies | D4. | Kept provisionally; time-zone arithmetic uses `Intl`. |\n   281\t\n   105\tThere is no `width`, `height`, `responsive`, `theme`, `palette` or `animation` option. Size comes from the container, colors from CSS, motion from the spec (I5). An unknown key raises AX150 with the closest known name.\n   106\t\n   107\t### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\n   108\t\n   109\t```ts\n   110\tchart.update({ title: 'Sessions' });                 // options\n   111\tchart.append([{ day: '2026-05-01', channel: 'Direct', sessions: 41210 }], { keep: 120 });   // rows\n   112\tchart.zoom(['2026-03-01', '2026-03-31']);            // view\n   113\tconst off = chart.on('hover', point => { … });\n   114\tchart.diagnostics;                                   // current, as soon as any call returns\n   115\tchart.destroy();\n   116\t```\n   117\t\n   118\tFinding D3: the ten libraries have seven update idioms. Axylo has three methods, one per kind of change, with one contract.\n   119\t\n   120\t| Rule | Detail |\n   121\t|---|---|\n   122\t| Order | Calls are applied in the order made. `update({ data })` followed by `append` appends to the new data. |\n   123\t| Two phases | Each call validates and normalizes synchronously: diagnostics are current when it returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call. Drawing is deferred to the next animation frame, and calls made before that frame are drawn once. |\n   124\t| Atomic switch | Marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer move to a new data revision together. No frame mixes two revisions. |\n   125\t| Patches | A key present in an `update` replaces the old value whole; a key set to `undefined` returns to its default; absent keys stay. No deep merging. |\n   126\t| Rejected changes | A call that throws under `strict` leaves the chart on its last valid state. |\n   127\t| Streaming | `keep` is the number of distinct x values retained. Rows before the last x are accepted through the slower full update and raise AX151. While zoomed to the latest edge the view follows new data; zoomed elsewhere, it stays put. Paused charts buffer (I6). Keyboard focus stays on its datum until that datum leaves the window (A7). |\n   128\t| Teardown | `destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects observers and listeners, and makes later calls no-ops. |\n   129\t\n   130\t### 1.6 Diagnostics\n   131\t\n   132\tThe registry, with each code's level and outcome, is in the spec (section 3.5). Example for the Phase 1 combined dirty fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`):\n   133\t\n   134\t```\n   135\t[axylo AX103] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" has 1 value that is not a finite number (NaN at row 3). Shown as a gap.\n   136\t  Check the calculation that produced it, or send null for \"no data\".\n   137\t[axylo AX106] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" mixes numbers with 1 string (\"40\" at row 4). The string is not plotted.\n   138\t  Convert it to a number where the data is produced.\n   139\t[axylo AX102] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" is absent in 1 row (row 5). Shown as a gap.\n   140\t  Send null if the value is known to be missing.\n   141\t```\n   142\t\n   143\tThe reader of that chart sees \"Values not shown: 3. See table.\" (F12).\n   144\t\n   145\t`validate(type, options)` runs the same normalization without a DOM and returns the diagnostics, so a server, a test or a coding agent can check a chart before rendering it.\n   146\t\n   147\tDevelopment and production flavours are selected through package `exports` conditions. The script-tag distribution ships `axylo.js` (development) and `axylo.min.js` (production). Errors and warnings print once per code per page in both; hints only in development.\n   148\t\n   169\t## 2. Architecture\n   170\t\n   171\t### 2.1 Pipeline\n   172\t\n   173\t```\n   174\toptions ─► normalize ─► frame ──┬─► view model ─► layout ─► scene ─► mark renderer\n   175\t            │ validate   (authoritative) │   scales, visible range         text renderer (DOM)\n   176\t            ▼ diagnostics                ├─► lookup: exact values by x, by point, by ordinal\n   177\t                                         ├─► summary and table\n   178\t                                         └─► focus model\n   179\t```\n   180\t\n   181\tThe **frame** is the normalized, lossless data: typed columns, a validity bitmap, dictionary-encoded categories, source-row indexes and a revision number. It is the only source for exact values. The table, the summary, keyboard navigation, tooltips and events read the frame. The **scene** is geometry for drawing and may be simplified (F5); nothing reads values back from it. The first draft derived accessibility from the scene, and two reviewers showed that a simplified drawing cannot return the samples it dropped.\n   182\t\n   183\t| Stage | Output | Notes |\n   184\t|---|---|---|\n   185\t| normalize | Frame, diagnostics | The only place raw input is read. Never coerces silently. |\n   186\t| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\n   187\t| lookup | Nearest-x by binary search; nearest-point by a grid index (scatter) | Works on the frame within the visible range; cost bounded independently of the drawing. |\n   188\t| layout | Plot rectangle, tick sets, label boxes, legend and control placement | Implements L1, L3, L8. Takes a text-measurement interface as input. |\n   189\t| scene | Flat list of primitives (`path`, `rect`, `arc`, `rule`, `text`) | Plain data; testable in Node with injected text metrics. |\n   190\t| renderers | Marks; DOM text; accessibility DOM | See 2.3. |\n   191\t\n   192\t### 2.2 Invalidation\n   193\t\n   194\tStages are cached, and the cache keys are explicit. Caching on object identity alone, as the first draft said, would miss font loads, pixel-ratio changes and in-place mutation.\n   195\t\n   196\t| Stage | Reruns when |\n   197\t|---|---|\n   198\t| normalize | Data revision or field encoding changes. `append` extends the frame without renormalizing earlier rows. |\n   199\t| view model | Frame revision, zoom, series visibility, author domains. |\n   200\t| layout | View model; container size; resolved font and text spacing; `document.fonts` finishing a load; locale; any label text. A data-only update reruns layout when the tick labels would change, and skips it otherwise. |\n   201\t| scene | Layout or view model. |\n   202\t| mark raster | Scene; device pixel ratio; resolved colors (L4's triggers). |\n   203\t\n   204\tText is measured with the page's computed font, letter spacing and word spacing applied to the measuring context, and layout reruns when fonts finish loading. The measurement interface is a parameter, so Node tests inject fixed metrics and browser tests use the real one.\n   205\t\n   338\t### M6. Updates and scale (ceiling: 10 days)\n   339\t\n   340\t- `update`, `append` and `zoom` under the full commit contract; pause; simplified drawing complete; zoom by drag, buttons, keyboard and opt-in touch.\n   341\t\n   342\tOutcomes:\n   343\t- Sequence tests from DX5.\n   344\t- F5's assertions on kept samples, including the adversarial same-column cases.\n   345\t- A value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000 that the drawing dropped is reachable by keyboard, tooltip and table.\n   346\t- Budgets P1–P5 and P7 measured with the Phase 3 method, recalibrated once if M1's provisional values were wrong, then frozen with a written reason for each.\n   347\t\n   348\tExamples: \"Live metrics\", \"A million points\". Docs: Updating, Large data, Zoom.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
