{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_116","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git diff e90d8db..9f9eeef -- docs/02-product-spec.md docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md'","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/docs/02-product-spec.md b/docs/02-product-spec.md\nindex 5f544d8..65953aa 100644\n--- a/docs/02-product-spec.md\n+++ b/docs/02-product-spec.md\n@@ -137,9 +137,9 @@ Target: every WCAG 2.2 level AA success criterion that applies to what Axylo ren\n |---|---|---|---|\n | A1 | Every chart has an accessible name: its `title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or else a generated name (\"Line chart of Sessions by Day\") with AX140 raised in every build. The title's heading level is the author's (`level`), and it can be hidden visually. Every chart has a generated summary: type, series, x range with time zone, y range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and any omissions. `description` adds to the summary and never removes the zone or omission statements. Element ids are unique per chart. | Accessibility-tree snapshot for every fixture; a 24-chart page has no duplicate ids; axe `heading-order` | R11, R13 |\n | A2 | Keyboard: at most two tab stops while the table is closed, the plot and one toolbar with roving focus that holds legend toggles, zoom, pause and the table control. Every point is reachable in order; the series can be switched; the ends can be reached directly; focus can leave the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px and 3:1 and is not hidden by the tooltip. The exact keys and roles are fixed after the pattern spike in M1 (section 8). | Forward and reverse traversal in collapsed, zoomed, streaming and table-open states; focus after hiding the focused series; indicator contrast | R12 |\n-| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's accessible name or a polite live region. On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key; a recorded rehearsal on NVDA with Chrome, NVDA with Firefox, VoiceOver with Safari on macOS, VoiceOver on iOS, and TalkBack with Chrome | R12, R13 |\n+| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's accessible name or a polite live region. On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key. No screen-reader rehearsal is part of 1.0 (the owner's decision, 2026-11-18): what a reader actually speaks on NVDA, VoiceOver or TalkBack has not been checked, and the conformance table says so. The script for such a rehearsal is kept in decision 0001 for when one is done. | R12, R13 |\n | A4 | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13 |\n-| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\n+| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. Bars are told apart by position and order, and by the legend and tooltip that name them; a compact chart has neither, so there neighbouring bar series also differ by fill pattern (series further apart may share a pattern: four patterns exist). The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\n | A6 | Content reflows at 320 CSS px and 400% zoom without two-dimensional scrolling. User text-spacing overrides do not clip text. | Viewport and style-override tests in three engines | WCAG 1.4.10, 1.4.12 |\n | A7 | Data arriving does not speak. Announcements happen only for the reader's own navigation, and for a change of state (L7). While data streams, keyboard focus stays on the same datum until it leaves the window. | Stream test counting live-region changes with and without focus; 24 concurrent streams | R12 |\n | A8 | axe-core reports no violations on any fixture in any state, including `heading-order` and `scrollable-region-focusable`. | CI | R11 |\n@@ -180,7 +180,7 @@ Level decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the ch\n | AX134 | Error | Series × x positions exceeds 20,000,000 (long rows with many series and little shared x) | Error state; the message gives both counts and suggests fewer series or a scatter chart |\n | AX152 | Warning | `timeZone` is not a zone the browser knows | The viewer's zone is used |\n | AX160 | Warning | A custom formatter threw | The default format is used for that value |\n-| AX199 | Error | Axylo itself failed while building the chart | Error state with a generic message; the error is passed to `onDiagnostic` and printed |\n+| AX199 | Error | An error was thrown while Axylo was building the chart: by Axylo itself, or by code the author supplied that Axylo was running (a row getter, a callback; a formatter that throws is AX160, and the default format is used) | Error state with a generic message; the error is passed to `onDiagnostic` and printed |\n \n Expected results for the Phase 1 fixtures: the combined `dirty` fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`) yields AX102, AX103 and AX106 once each, draws Mon–Tue as a segment and Sun as a point, and shows the F12 note. `dirty-string` (one `'40'` among numbers) yields AX106. A column made only of numeric strings yields AX104 and is drawn in full. `dirty-null` yields nothing.\n \n@@ -214,7 +214,7 @@ A compact chart does not give access to every value; the summary is its alternat\n \n ### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\n \n-Automated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. The only evidence for that in 1.0 is A3's rehearsal by the developer on five reader and browser pairs, recorded as rehearsal. Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says so in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\n+Automated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. 1.0 has no evidence for that: no screen reader was run against it, not even by the developer (the owner's decision, 2026-11-18; the rehearsal on five reader and browser pairs that earlier drafts of this section planned was not done). Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says both things in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\n \n ## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\n \n@@ -226,7 +226,7 @@ Automated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text.\n | DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\n | DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\n | DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\n-| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets, set by the owner on 2026-11-18 after measurement: 96 KiB gzipped for everything and 80 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, CSS included. (The first targets, 40 and 25 KiB, were written before anything was built; an analysis of the built library showed they cannot hold the accessibility layer, the table, diagnostics, three kinds of time and streaming: `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size.analysis.md`.) | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\n+| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets for 1.0, set by the owner on 2026-11-18 after measurement: 116 KiB gzipped for everything and 98 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, CSS included. (History: the first targets, 40 and 25 KiB, were written before anything was built; 96 and 80 were set the same day from a first analysis, `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size.analysis.md`; the correctness passes that followed added about 11 KiB, and a second measured analysis, `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size2.analysis.md`, showed 96 and 80 can only be met by making streaming an opt-in import, a public API change the owner left for after 1.0. That change, worth about 15 KiB, is the first size item after 1.0.) | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\n | DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element works and leaks nothing (the sequence a framework's strict or development mode produces). Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\n | DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\n | DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\n@@ -283,7 +283,7 @@ Three rules hold whatever the numbers become:\n \n | Proposal | Why not |\n |---|---|\n-| Test with screen-reader users, outside developers or design partners before release | One-person project with no participants. Recorded as a stated limit (3.8). Agent rehearsal (DX12) and developer rehearsal (A3) are used and labelled as such. |\n+| Test with screen-reader users, outside developers or design partners before release | One-person project with no participants. Recorded as a stated limit (3.8). Agent rehearsal (DX12) is used and labelled as such; the developer's screen-reader rehearsal (A3) was dropped from 1.0 by the owner and is stated as not done. |\n | Collect chart sizes and device profiles from beta deployments | A chart library should not phone home. Workload sizes come from the spec and from issue reports. |\n | Switch to horizontal bars automatically when labels do not fit | Changing orientation changes what the chart says at a glance; Axylo suggests it and tells the reader what is hidden. |\n | Remove the first-chart line-count criterion | Kept as a floor, and joined by DX12, which tests getting it right. |\ndiff --git a/docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md b/docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md\nindex 5ecd3dc..596141d 100644\n--- a/docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md\n+++ b/docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md\n@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@ Revision 1 (placed across the reading axis, offset from the pointer, interactive\n - *A key that leaves the reader on the same datum* (an arrow at the end of the data, Home on the first point) is not a move: it does not release a pin (rule 4) and does not rewrite the focused text (part 2).\n - *A gesture and a pin:* starting a drag or a pinch ends the pin. No state exists in which a pin is held and nothing is shown.\n - *A drag while Select range is pressed* zooms as a drag and releases the toggle: the range has been chosen.\n-- *After Escape from a hold* nothing opens at once: the held datum and the datum under the still pointer both stay closed until the pointer reaches another datum. The pick itself is current, so a click activates what is under the pointer.\n+- *After Escape from a hold* nothing opens at once: the held datum and the datum under the still pointer are both dismissed. A dismissed datum stays closed until the pointer reaches a datum other than itself (so after moving from one of the two onto the other, that other opens). Escape from a pin or from a plain hover dismisses only the datum on show, not whatever else the pointer is over. The pick itself is always current, so a click activates what is under the pointer.\n - *The plot's focus ring* shows when the plot has focus and the last input to it was a key it handles, or when the browser matches `:focus-visible` (which the three engines do after a click on the plot as well). A ring after a click is accepted; what is required is that keyboard use always shows it.\n - *Pointer positions are known to one CSS pixel.* Firefox and WebKit report mouse positions in whole pixels (a pointer at 336.96, 82.27 arrives as 336, 82), while the plot's edges and the corridor's edges are fractional. A position up to 1 CSS px outside the plot rectangle is therefore picked as if on its edge, and the corridor of rule 3b is taken with 1 px of slack, so a mark drawn on the plot's edge can be pointed at and a move along the corridor's edge stays inside it in every engine.\n \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
